NIGHT KITCHEN
by a filmmaker who still has a day job

LOGLINE
A night-shift cook in Surat keeps her late mother's recipe book taped under a prep table. When a food-show producer wants to "elevate" the dish for a national trailer, she has to decide whether the kissa of that kitchen survives being clipped.

INT. SURAT DHABA KITCHEN - NIGHT

Steam. A radio no one changes. MEENA (34) plates undhiyu like she is closing a wound. She glances at the underside of the steel table. A cloth-bound notebook is taped there with surgical tape.

MEENA
Not for camera.

RAVI (22), porter, watches the back door. A van idles.

INT. DHABA FRONT - NIGHT

ARJUN (40s), a producer with a ring light in his tote, tastes with a performer face.

ARJUN
If we shoot the pour in slow motion, this becomes a clip. That's the business now.

MEENA
The pour is not the story.

INT. MEENA'S ROOM ABOVE THE DHABA - DAWN

The notebook open. Her mother's handwriting in Gujarati. A stain that is not spice.

MEENA (V.O.)
She wrote the heat as a feeling, not a number.

EXT. TEXTILE MARKET - DAY

Arjun films vendors without asking. A watchman waves him off. Ravi translates badly on purpose.

INT. HOSPITAL CORRIDOR - NIGHT

A memory. Meena's mother, ANJALI, counts pills into a steel katori. No score. A fluorescent blink.

ANJALI
If they put it on the phone, they will forget the smell.

INT. DHABA KITCHEN - NIGHT

Meena cooks the dish once, correctly, with the radio off. She does not plate for the camera. She plates for the empty stool.

ARJUN
We can add a drone over the river.

MEENA
There is no river in this kitchen.

FADE OUT.
